## Adapter Setup

The generated client depends only on the `{% if is_rpc %}CratestackRpcAdapter{% else %}CratestackClientAdapter{% endif %}` interface. The built-in implementation is `{% if is_rpc %}CratestackRpcHttpAdapter{% else %}CratestackHttpAdapter{% endif %}`, written once over a pure-Dart HTTP seam, `CratestackHttpTransport`. The adapter owns the headers, the CBOR/JSON choice, the response `Content-Type` dispatch{% if not is_rpc %}, the canonical query string{% endif %} and the error mapping; the transport owns the connection.

{% if is_fespalier_preset %}
This package sends through fespalier's `HttpTransport` (`package:fespalier_transport`, a git dependency pinned at `{{ fespalier_ref }}`; it is not on pub.dev, and `dio` is not a dependency). `CratestackFespalierTransport`, in `lib/src/fespalier_transport.dart`, adapts it to the seam:

```dart
final adapter = {% if is_rpc %}CratestackRpcHttpAdapter{% else %}CratestackHttpAdapter{% endif %}(
  CratestackFespalierTransport(
    // Your transport chain, outer to inner. A signer sees the exact CBOR bytes
    // that are sent.
    SessionTransport(WriteGuardTransport(HttpClientTransport(client)), credentials),
  ),
  baseUri: Uri.parse('https://api.example.com'),
{% if not is_rpc %}
  // useRustTransport: true,   // marks requests for a host interceptor that routes them through Rust
{% endif %}
);

final client = {{ client_class_name }}(
  adapter,
{% if not is_rpc %}
  basePath: '{{ base_path_literal }}',
{% endif %}
);
```

`baseUri` is joined with the request path (a path prefix such as `https://example.com/v1` is kept). What `CratestackFespalierTransport` maps:

- the request: the exact body bytes, the headers, and `CratestackHttpRequest.extra` as `TransportRequest.attributes` (never sent). The abort is the call option's `abort` merged with the abort trigger of the current `TransportZone` (`TransportZone.merge`), so either one aborts: `ref.cancellable` in `fespalier_cratestack` aborts a page's loads with no extra wiring, even for a call that carries its own abort.
- the answer: every status is a response, which the adapter maps to the typed error. A `TransportStatusError` thrown by a decorator is treated as the answer it carries.
- a call that got no answer: `TransportAborted`, `TransportUnsent`, `TransportTimedOut` and `TransportBroken` map one to one onto `CratestackTransportAborted`, `Unsent`, `TimedOut` and `Broken`. `TransportPortal` and `TransportUntrusted` become `CratestackTransportBroken`; its `cause` is the original failure, so check `error.cause is TransportPortal` to tell them apart. Any other error a decorator throws (such as `AuthRejected`) reaches the caller unchanged.

fespalier packages are git dependencies by design, so the pinned ref must be reachable for this package to resolve. `--fespalier-ref` changes it; where it is not reachable yet (an unreleased fespalier), point `fespalier_transport` at a local checkout with a `pubspec_overrides.yaml`.
{% else %}
Dio is one transport (`CratestackDioTransport`, in `lib/src/dio_transport.dart`, the only file that names Dio):

```dart
final adapter = {% if is_rpc %}CratestackRpcHttpAdapter{% else %}CratestackHttpAdapter{% endif %}(
  CratestackDioTransport(myDio),
  baseUri: Uri.parse('https://api.example.com'),
{% if not is_rpc %}
  // useRustTransport: true,   // marks requests for a host interceptor that routes them through Rust
{% endif %}
);

final client = {{ client_class_name }}(
  adapter,
{% if not is_rpc %}
  basePath: '{{ base_path_literal }}',
{% endif %}
);
```

`baseUri` is joined with the request path (a path prefix such as `https://example.com/v1` is kept), so `myDio.options.baseUrl` is not used. Interceptors, base headers, certificate pinning and retry on `myDio` keep working.

{% endif %}
Any other HTTP stack implements `CratestackHttpTransport` (one method, `send`) and is handed to the same adapter. Resolve with the response for every HTTP status; throw a `CratestackTransportException` (`Aborted`, `Unsent`, `TimedOut` or `Broken`) when there is no answer:

```dart
class MyTransport implements CratestackHttpTransport {
  @override
  Future<CratestackHttpResponse> send(CratestackHttpRequest request) async {
    // request.method, request.uri, request.headers, request.body (the exact
    // bytes to send), request.abort (completes when the caller gives up).
    throw UnimplementedError();
  }
}
```

Wire format:

- `format: CratestackWireFormat.cbor` (the default) writes CBOR bodies and prefers a CBOR answer
- `format: CratestackWireFormat.json` writes JSON bodies and prefers a JSON answer
- either way the answer is decoded by its own `Content-Type`

Errors:

{% if is_rpc %}
- an HTTP error answer is a `CratestackRpcException` (`status`, `code`, `message`, `details`, `extra` and `challenges`)
{% else %}
- an HTTP error answer is a `CratestackRestException` (`status`, the decoded `body`, and its typed `code`, `message`, `details`, `extra` and `challenges`)
{% endif %}
- a refusal from the server's `AuthProvider` keeps its fixed code; the members it adds (`{"error": "attestation_expired"}`) are in `extra`, and its `WWW-Authenticate` value, raw, is in `challenges`
- a call that got no answer is a `CratestackTransportException`: `CratestackTransportUnsent` (provably never left the device, always safe to resend), `CratestackTransportAborted`, `CratestackTransportTimedOut` or `CratestackTransportBroken`. Never a Dio type.

Cancelling a call:

```dart
final cancel = Completer<void>();
final future = client.procedures.someCall(
  args,
  options: {% if is_rpc %}CratestackRpcCallOptions{% else %}CratestackCallOptions{% endif %}(abort: cancel.future),
);
cancel.complete(); // the call throws CratestackTransportAborted
```

Per-request headers:

```dart
const options = {% if is_rpc %}CratestackRpcCallOptions{% else %}CratestackCallOptions{% endif %}(
  headers: {'x-auth-id': '1'},
  idempotencyKey: 'order-42', // sent as Idempotency-Key
);
```

